hi there steve kaufman here today i'm  going to talk about accents because  someone i can't remember where or maybe  on google plus or here at youtube asked  me  um  you know which accent should we learn  and it's a question that i get all the  time or which accent do i prefer uh and  so i want to take talk a little bit  about the subject of accents  um and i'm sorry that i don't plan these  out ahead of time but some of the basic  principles that that you know affect my  attitude towards accent is that number  one it doesn't matter  number two um  i guess we  will learn the accent  that we find the most attractive  or the most useful  or the most prestigious or for whatever  reasons that we have  or we'll learn the accent that we are  exposed to the most and it really  doesn't matter so if i go through the  languages that i speak starting with say  you know in order of sort of declining  order of proficiency  uh in case of french even though i grew  up in montreal  and i had french at school i didn't  really speak much french in montreal and  then i went to france where i studied  for three years so as a consequence i  ended up speaking more  like they do in france  uh when i am around people from quebec  my accent starts to migrate a bit a bit  and if i were to live in montreal and i  dealt on a daily basis with people from  montreal or from quebec my accent would  move more towards the accent of  people around me and i think that in i  have found at any rate that in our  in the languages that we learn as  opposed to our native language that we  are easily influenced by the people  we're speaking with in our native  language  it seems to be better anchored and our  our pronunciation doesn't change i mean  i've met obviously you know british  people living in canada who have lived  here for 50 years and their accent  hasn't changed some of them their accent  changes but most of them they don't  uh but when it comes to  uh a foreign language i find that i'm  influenced when i lived in france as a  student if i was down south pretty soon  i'd be starting to talk a little bit  like the people there  so i i think that the accent the  pronunciation that we have in another  language is never anchored quite as  strongly as it is in our first language  and so i mean i right now i speak the  way i speak because most of my contacts  have been with people from france and it  continues to be because i go to france  more often than i go to quebec  which should you learn obviously if  you're gonna i would think in the case  of french if you're gonna be living in  quebec you should learn the uh quebecois  accent uh if you're gonna be living in  france you learn the french accent if  you lived uh you know in the south of  france you'd to perhaps speak the way  they do i don't know  so then we get from there to japanese  uh definitely my japanese  while it's it's largely sort of  standard tokyo japanese because i in the  lumber business i had a lot of dealing  dealings with people from the kansai  from osaka  there are people who notice certain  kansai type intonations in my japanese  it's not a full-blown kansai accent but  they're certainly just by having spent  so much time with people  from the khan side that has influenced  my  japanese but i made no particular effort  to speak this way or that way i just  find that you you sort of pick it up  similarly with chinese my teachers were  from beijing so i have more of a beijing  accent but someone who lives in taiwan  or lives around shanghai will probably  pick up the accent there and it really  doesn't matter and as i say i think it's  quite fluid if you move to an area where  a different accent is used you'll  probably uh pick that up  spanish  again i learned most of my spanish in  spain and i happen to like the spanish  accent i like the use of the thu you  know so that a soft c  or a z is pronounced like a th sound  in south america they don't do that  i like the accent in mexico because it's  quite easy to understand it has a little  more of a sing-song in some ways it's  easier to understand than the more  machine gun  spanish in spain  but  i like having that  sound to hang on to because it tells me  whether it's an s or a c  and i've observed that a lot of people  in latin america make spelling mistakes  because they don't know whether it's an  s or a c so i kind of like the the sound  my wife doesn't like it at all her  mother was costa rican and she doesn't  like it when we go to spain and she  won't pronounce you know  uh  gunfall  she likes to say gonzalez  so it really doesn't matter it's  whatever you like while we're on the  subject of spanish no and i like the  argentinian accent i think it's kind of  neat to listen to it's got a real nice  cachet to it uh  chilean i find very difficult to  understand they chew all their words  but if i lived there i'd probably end up  speaking more like a chilean  so  and a lot of people sort of say well you  know in learning a language i only want  to listen to you know this form of  english and i think i've never had that  problem at all  i'm interested in learning words so i'm  listening to stuff that i'm reading i'm  using link to learn words and so in the  case of portuguese i couldn't care less  whether it was european portuguese or  brazilian portuguese in fact i  deliberately well mostly i listened to  things of interest but i also wanted to  be exposed to both european portuguese  and brazilian portuguese while learning  the words which are overwhelmingly the  same  and just so that i could in fact  communicate with both and i suspect  because my portuguese isn't very strong  that when i'm in  probably my portuguese accent is  influenced more than anything by my  spanish  so i speak a kind of portugal mixture of  portuguese and espanol  and  leaning a little more towards portuguese  pronunciation when i'm in portugal and  if i were to spend a few weeks in brazil  i would probably move in the other  direction  uh accents are interesting german for  example i guess i tend to speak more of  a northern german pronunciation i guess  closer to  obviously don't sound like german i  sound like a foreigner but  and yet but i like the bavarian accent i  like the uh austrian accent  uh  again as i say if i live there i'd  probably be influenced by it swedish is  an interesting thing with with access  like  russian which i have not  even though i listen to a lot of russian  i've got i probably have a bigger  vocabulary in russian than in swedish  but in russian i cannot tell  anybody's accent i don't know whether  they're from saint petersburg moscow or  kutsk wherever except if they're from  ukraine i can tell because the g is  pronounced like an h and that's about  the only thing that i can identify with  okay swedish  if when i'm in a certain area like  dalaina in sweden  or smalland and i spent a lot of time in  both dolena and smolland on my lumber  business  there you know smallant has an accent  dalara has an accent but the interesting  thing is that within dalana each village  has an accent and the same is molan so i  was at the 50th birthday party for one  of the people that we deal with on the  lumber business and a lot of people from  his village came to the birthday party  and i noticed that while they were all  speaking this small and accent theirs  was a little different so it's very  interesting how people living in this  little village will be influenced by  what they hear around them the same in  donna three villa or three different  towns mura lakes and uh  you know uh  what was the other town there i can't  remember  anyway my memory's gone but but  definitely the distinct accents within  the sort of micro accents within the  larger accents which i've definitely  picked up in swedish uh  almost more than anywhere else in  swedish i really really noticed it  so um yeah i don't think accents matter  that much it's  language is more about words and  vocabulary getting used to the language  and you're you can always if you  particularly like a kind of accent you  can listen to that and give yourself  sort of a  overdose of that accent and you will  migrate towards that accent people who  say that you know if you listen to the  wrong accent or whatever in your uh  early stages you'll never get rid of  that i don't believe that i find that  we're we're very very  easily influenced by the accent of  people around us  so  there's my take uh no real conclusion i  think people put too much  importance on this issue of accent learn  the accent you want to learn but don't  listen just to that accent  if you want to expand your your  familiarity with the language but if you  want to really train yourself to speak a  certain way then obviously you want to  find just that accent  and for learning purposes find a voice  you like and listen to content you like  and the more you like it the better  you're gonna learn  and that's all i have to say thanks for  now for listening bye for now
 

